Q: Input loses focus after disabled and re-enabled A: <p>You can add a clientside callback that will use javascript to grab the <code>input</code> element and set focus when the input is re-enabled.</p>
<pre><code>app.clientside_callback(
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    function(disabled) {
        if (disabled === false) {
            const el = document.getElementById('input');
            if (el) {
                el.focus();
            }
        }
        return window.dash_clientside.no_update;
    }
    &quot;&quot;&quot;,
    Input('input', 'disabled')
)

</code></pre>
<p>After the input is re-enabled, the input doesn't lose its focus mode:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/82lJe1tTm.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/82lJe1tTm.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>

Q: Why is there no `orient=table` option for `pandas.DataFrame.to_dict`? A: <p>Short answer: <code>orient='table'</code> in <code>to_json</code> isn't just another dict layout - it generates a <a href="https://specs.frictionlessdata.io/table-schema/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JSON Table Schema</a> with dtype metadata (<code>schema.fields[].type</code>), primary key info, etc. That schema generation logic lives in <code>pandas.io.json._table_schema</code>, completely separate from <code>to_dict</code>'s orient machinery. Adding it to <code>to_dict</code> would mean pulling in that entire schema-building pipeline for what's supposed to be a lightweight dict conversion.</p>
<p>That said, you don't need the <code>json.loads(to_json(...))</code> roundtrip. You can build the table dict directly:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>from pandas.io.json import build_table_schema

dict_to_write = {
    'additionalData': ...,
    'table': {
        'schema': build_table_schema(df),
        'data': df.to_dict(orient='records'),
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>build_table_schema</code> gives you the schema part as a dict (not a string), and <code>to_dict(orient='records')</code> gives you the data rows. No serialization-then-parsing needed.</p>
<p>If you want it to match <code>to_json(orient='table')</code> exactly (including the <code>pandas_version</code> field), the roundtrip via <code>json.loads</code> is the only way to get a 1:1 match, since some of that metadata is added during the JSON serialization step. But for most use cases the above is equivalent and cleaner.</p>

Q: iPadOS 26 Crash when floating num pad in presented view A: <p>This is a UIKit bug, not your code. Here's what's happening under the hood:</p>
<p>The floating numpad on iPad lives in a separate <code>UIRemoteKeyboardWindow</code>. When you tap outside the textfield in a <code>.sheet</code>, UIKit tries to create a layout constraint between <code>_UIRemoteKeyboardPlaceholderView</code> (keyboard window) and a view inside your sheet's window. Two different windows = no common ancestor = crash. It's a classic cross-window constraint violation that Apple's internal code is triggering, not yours.</p>
<p>The existing answer says &quot;avoid <code>.sheet</code> or <code>.numberPad</code>&quot; - that's technically true but not practical if you have a real app. Here's what actually works:</p>
<p><strong>Intercept the tap yourself with <code>@FocusState</code>:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct PresentedView: View {
    @State private var text = &quot;&quot;
    @FocusState private var isFieldFocused: Bool

    var body: some View {
        ScrollView {
            VStack {
                TextField(&quot;Placeholder&quot;, text: $text)
                    .keyboardType(.numberPad)
                    .focused($isFieldFocused)
            }
            .padding(80)
        }
        .contentShape(Rectangle())
        .onTapGesture {
            isFieldFocused = false
        }
        .toolbar {
            ToolbarItemGroup(placement: .keyboard) {
                Spacer()
                Button(&quot;Done&quot;) { isFieldFocused = false }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The key insight: by catching the tap-outside gesture yourself and setting <code>isFieldFocused = false</code>, you force a clean <code>resignFirstResponder</code> <em>before</em> UIKit's sheet dismissal handler tries to reconcile the keyboard constraints across windows. That race condition is what causes the crash.</p>
<p>If this still acts up on your specific device/OS combo (I've seen inconsistencies between simulator and hardware), your nuclear option is wrapping a <code>UITextField</code> in <code>UIViewRepresentable</code> - gives you full control over responder timing and bypasses SwiftUI's keyboard handling entirely.</p>
<p>Either way, file a Feedback at <a href="https://feedbackassistant.apple.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://feedbackassistant.apple.com</a>. The 26.0.1 patch only fixed the &quot;keyboard jumping around&quot; visual bug, not this constraint crash. It needs a proper fix from Apple's side.</p>

Q: `pgoutput` does not send BEGIN/COMMIT for empty transactions in PostgreSQL 18 — replication slot LSN does not advance A: <p>The behavior you're hitting was introduced in PostgreSQL 15 by <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/commit/d5a9d86d8f" rel="nofollow noreferrer">commit d5a9d86</a> - <code>pgoutput</code> now postpones the BEGIN until the first actual published change arrives, and if none does, it skips COMMIT too. The whole point was to save CPU/bandwidth on empty transactions that the subscriber doesn't care about.</p>
<p>You said PG17 worked - that's likely because your heartbeat table was part of the publication in that setup (intentionally or via <code>FOR ALL TABLES</code>), or the slot was created with a different config. The skipping has been active since PG15 for transactions that touch <em>only</em> non-published tables. Worth double-checking your old PG17 publication definition.</p>
<p>Either way, the fix:</p>
<p><strong>Option 1: Add the heartbeat table to the publication</strong></p>
<p>The simplest approach. If <code>pgoutput</code> sees a DML on a published table, it sends BEGIN + the change + COMMIT, and your existing <code>txMsgsCnt</code> logic works:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>ALTER PUBLICATION my_pub ADD TABLE heartbeat;
</code></pre>
<p>Your consumer will now see these as non-empty transactions (1 INSERT message). Filter them out on the consumer side by checking the relation name, and use the commit LSN to advance the slot. This is the most reliable approach because it works with any PG version and doesn't require changing your replication stream logic.</p>
<p><strong>Option 2: Use <code>pg_logical_emit_message()</code> instead of a heartbeat table</strong></p>
<p>This function emits a logical decoding message directly into the WAL stream. With <code>transactional := true</code>, it gets wrapped in a BEGIN/COMMIT pair and shows up as a <code>Message</code> event in your stream - even if no published table was touched:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>SELECT pg_logical_emit_message(true, 'heartbeat', 'ping');
</code></pre>
<p>Your <code>PGReplicationStream</code> will receive this as a <code>LogicalDecodingMessage</code> between <code>TxBeginEvent</code>/<code>TxCommitEvent</code>. You'd need to handle this message type in your consumer code - treat it as a non-empty transaction and use its LSN for flushing.</p>
<p>The advantage over option 1 is no actual table writes, no bloat, no vacuuming. The disadvantage is you need to update your consumer to recognize the message type.</p>
<p><strong>Option 3: Flush based on keepalive LSN, not transaction boundaries</strong></p>
<p>This is the most robust long-term approach but requires rethinking your consumer. Instead of waiting for BEGIN/COMMIT and counting DMLs, periodically flush based on the last LSN received from the stream - including keepalive messages.</p>
<p><code>PGReplicationStream.getLastReceiveLSN()</code> gives you the server's current position even when no data messages are flowing. You can flush this on a timer:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>// In your polling loop, regardless of whether you got data:
LogSequenceNumber serverLsn = stream.getLastReceiveLSN();
if (serverLsn != null &amp;&amp; serverLsn.asLong() &gt; lastFlushedLsn.asLong()) {
    stream.setAppliedLSN(serverLsn);
    stream.setFlushedLSN(serverLsn);
    stream.forceUpdateStatus();
    lastFlushedLsn = serverLsn;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This way you don't depend on seeing any particular message type at all. The slot advances even during periods of pure empty transactions.</p>
<p><strong>My recommendation:</strong> Option 1 if you want a 5-minute fix. Option 3 if you want to stop fighting this class of problem permanently.</p>
